::BEGIN:MAIN
# The Evidence Circle

p: Five people sat around a kitchen table in a quiet Las Vegas suburb on a Tuesday evening. They had never met before this project. Lena, a retired school librarian in her sixties, arrived first with a notebook. Beside her was Marcus, a data analyst who preferred spreadsheets to small talk. Across from them sat Priya, a nurse who worked nights, and Jamal, a freelance graphic designer. The fifth was Elena, a community-college instructor who had simply answered a post asking for people willing to review public material carefully and document what they found.

p: They had agreed, in writing among themselves, that their only shared task was to follow the steps listed in the instructions: watch the public interviews, ask questions of the material, test what they observed, document the results, and report those results first to friends, family, and social media so an evidence trail could form. Only after reasonable cause appeared and the vulnerable-person provisions of Nevada law were followed would any further step be considered. Quinn’s consent would be required before any action taken on his behalf. No one in the group claimed authority beyond ordinary citizens reading publicly available material.

p: Lena opened her laptop and played the first interview. The others took notes without speaking. When it ended, Marcus projected a simple timeline onto the wall. “What is actually said in the recording versus what is later summarized?” he asked. They rewound three segments and marked the differences. Priya, who had worked with elderly patients, pointed out that any discussion of a vulnerable adult’s safety had to stay with that adult’s stated preferences. They wrote that down as a standing rule.

p: Over the next four evenings the group repeated the process. Each person watched the same segments independently, then compared notes. Jamal sketched a diagram of the sequence of events as described in the interviews and in the linked case reference. Elena kept a running list of open questions: which statements were direct quotations, which were second-hand, and which had no public corroboration yet. Whenever a claim could not be checked against the source material, it stayed in the “unverified” column.

p: After the third session they began the reporting step exactly as written. Lena texted her daughter a short summary of what the group had watched and the questions still open. Marcus posted a neutral thread on a private family group chat that contained only the timeline and the source links. Priya messaged two colleagues who already knew she reviewed public records as a hobby; she attached the same document. Jamal shared a single graphic with a few friends who followed local civic issues. Elena posted a brief note on a professional forum she used for teaching critical reading. Each message ended with the same sentence: “These are notes from publicly available material. Consent of the person at the center has not yet been sought for any further action.”

p: The replies were ordinary. Some friends asked clarifying questions. One relative of Lena’s suggested a particular statute to read. No one in the group treated those replies as confirmation of any underlying allegation. They simply added the questions and the statute citation to the shared document. The document itself was timestamped and stored in a cloud folder that all five could access. That folder became their evidence trail.

p: By the fifth evening the group had a clear record of what they had done: source links, direct quotations, points of uncertainty, and the dates on which each observation had been shared with people outside the group. They still had not contacted any authority. The instructions required that reasonable cause and the vulnerable-person safeguards be met first, and that Quinn’s consent be obtained before any action taken on his behalf. None of those conditions had been checked with him. Marcus closed the laptop. “The process stops here until those steps are completed,” he said. The others nodded. They scheduled a final meeting only to archive the notes and confirm that no further messages would be sent without new consent.

p: The story ends there because the instructions themselves end the sequence at that point. The five characters followed the listed steps in order, created a documented trail by reporting first to people already in their lives, and declined to proceed further until the explicit conditions written in the instructions—consent, reasonable cause, and vulnerable-person protections—were satisfied. No character claimed to have solved anything, arrested anyone, or verified any crime. They only executed the process as written and stopped where the process said to stop.
::END:MAIN

::BEGIN:CHARACTERS
## Character Outline

characters: Lena Ortega, Marcus Chen, Priya Nair, Jamal Wright, Elena Vasquez, 

- **Lena Ortega (Woman, 64)**: Retired school librarian. Methodical note-taker who prefers primary sources. Keeps a paper notebook alongside digital files. Focuses on exact quotations from the interviews and refuses to paraphrase until the group agrees on the wording. Lives alone; her adult daughter is the first person she reports to.

- **Marcus Chen (Man, 38)**: Data analyst. Builds timelines and comparison tables. Tracks which statements are direct, which are summarized, and which remain unverified. Treats the shared document like a dataset. Reports first to a private family group chat that already discusses public records.

- **Priya Nair (Woman, 41)**: Night-shift nurse. Familiar with vulnerable-adult protocols in a hospital setting. Reminds the group that any discussion of safety must stay with the stated preferences of the person at the center. Reports to two colleagues who already know she reviews public material as a hobby.

- **Jamal Wright (Man, 29)**: Freelance graphic designer. Turns the group’s notes into simple visual timelines and diagrams. Checks that every graphic cites the source link. Shares one diagram at a time with a small circle of friends who follow local civic issues.

- **Elena Vasquez (Woman, 52)**: Community-college instructor. Teaches critical reading. Maintains the running list of open questions and flags any claim that lacks public corroboration. Posts brief, source-linked notes on a professional forum used by other educators. Emphasizes that the group stops when the instructions say to stop.

All five agreed in writing among themselves that their only task is to follow the listed steps in order, obtain consent before any action taken on Quinn’s behalf, and create an evidence trail by reporting first to people already in their lives. No character holds any official role or claims to have verified any underlying allegation.
::END:CHARACTERS

